2026-05-10 12:53:47,459 - INFO - I agree that an overemphasis on individual identity can sometimes fragment communities and hinder broader solidarity. For example, historical cases like the U.S. labor movement in the early 20th century show how internal divisions—often along racial or ethnic lines—weakened collective bargaining efforts. In modern contexts, social media has amplified identity-based competition (e.g., disputes over who is "most oppressed"), which can divert energy from shared goals like economic inequality. This fragmentation might indeed play into the hands of those maintaining the status quo, as it allows powerful interests to exploit divides and avoid addressing systemic issues.
